$en3$There is one obligation in the new BSIG that applies even to financial institutions relying entirely on DORA. Just one. And it is simpler than anything else in the statute: you must register with the BSI. No exceptions. No DORA exemption. No deferral.

Since 6 December 2025, the NIS2 Implementation Act has been in force. Section 28(6) BSIG clarifies that DORA-regulated financial undertakings are exempt from the substantive obligations of Sections 30 through 39 BSIG. What many overlook: this exemption expressly does not extend to the registration obligation under Section 33 BSIG. The BSI has confirmed this in its FAQ: yes, DORA-regulated entities must register with the BSI.

As of February 2026, only approximately 1,900 entities have registered with the BSI. This represents fewer than ten percent of the roughly 29,500 obligated undertakings in Germany. The registration deadline expired on 6 March 2026. Any entity that remains unregistered is therefore in default.

| DORA exempts financial undertakings from nearly all BSIG obligations. But not from Section 33. This one obligation remains — and nine out of ten financial institutions are ignoring it. |

I. What Section 33 BSIG Specifically Requires

Section 33(1) BSIG obliges essential and important entities to register with the BSI no later than three months after first becoming subject to the statute. For undertakings already subject on 6 December 2025, this deadline expired on 6 March 2026.

Registration is conducted through the BSI portal at portal.bsi.bund.de, operational since 6 January 2026. Prerequisites include an ELSTER organisational certificate and a Mein Unternehmenskonto (MUK) account. The process has two stages: first create a MUK account, then register on the BSI portal.

Required information includes: full entity name, address and contact person, relevant sectors under Annex 1 or 2 BSIG, public IP address ranges, and a 24/7 reachable contact point for security incidents.

II. Why the DORA Exemption Does Not Apply Here

Section 28(6) BSIG exhaustively lists the exempted sections: Sections 30, 31, 32, 35, 36, 38, 39. Section 33 is not on this list. It applies without restriction to DORA-regulated financial undertakings.

In practice: a bank that has been DORA-compliant since January 2025 can still receive a fine under Section 65 BSIG — solely for failing to register with the BSI. Fines for non-registration can amount to up to EUR 500,000.

III. The Group Question: Who Must Register?

In financial groups, a single group-wide registration does not suffice. Each legal entity that independently meets the criteria must register separately.

Particularly relevant: an intra-group IT company serving both DORA-regulated and NIS2-relevant entities is itself subject to the registration obligation. The DORA exemption applies only to the financial undertaking itself — not to its IT subsidiary.

| Consequences of failure to register: fine under Section 65 BSIG of up to EUR 500,000. The BSI may carry out registration ex officio. Without registration, no access to the BSI portal — incidents must be reported via emergency form. |

IV. What Needs to Be Done Now

If registration has not yet been completed, it must be done without delay. The 6 March 2026 deadline has passed, but prompt action can mitigate the omission. The BSI has signalled that it initially prioritises cooperation.

For groups: all potentially affected entities should be identified centrally. Registration of each individual entity must then be completed separately.

